From 32920fcc2a0090f18512166468b85f8bf9b92bc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StefanVonRanda <16295563+StefanVonRanda@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:58:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Add transparent PNG output (--rgba)

paralaif/MOSS-Image-Alpha-LoRA teaches MOSS-Image to put an alpha signal
into its latents; trmz/flux2-klein-alpha's RGBA VAE (same latent space and
batch-norm stats as FLUX.2's) decodes it to a fourth channel. moss-mlx's
decoder was hard-wired to 3 output channels.

- vae.py: Decoder takes out_channels from the VAE config; rgba_vae_dir()
  fetches the RGBA VAE (config + weights) from the Hub on first use.
- pipeline.py: Request.rgba decodes with that VAE (the pipeline reloads the
  decoder only when the kind changes); inpainting pads its kept pixels with
  opaque alpha.
- cli.py: --rgba.
The LoRA's fused qkv keys load through the LoRA key-format commit.

Checked: pytest 40 passed (a 4-channel toy decoder). Real run on 0.2.0,
compact tier, 8-step DMD + moss_alpha_v1, CFG 1, 1024^2, "A small red fox
wizard with a glass potion bottle, full body, isolated on a transparent
background": RGBA PNG, 80 % of pixels alpha < 10, 16 % > 245; composited
on green the fox is cleanly cut out and the bottle is translucent; 32 s
denoise, 8.6 GB MLX peak.
Not checked: rgba together with inpainting on real weights.
The alpha LoRA is licensed for non-commercial research only.
